coin-inertia-ask = Why does the coin drop into the glass when the card is snatched away?
coin-inertia-title = Inertia: a coin on a card
coin-inertia-question = A glass is covered with a postcard, a coin on the card over the glass’s mouth. We take the card out sideways in four ways: draw it slowly and smoothly, push it with a finger, push it sharply and flick it. We film it on a phone and measure by the frames how far the coin moved while the card went from under it. Why does the coin drop into the glass when the card is snatched away?
coin-inertia-choices = The coin keeps its state of rest: the card goes so fast that friction has no time to move the coin
coin-inertia-choices-2 = The coin is heavier than the card, so it drops straight down
coin-inertia-choices-3 = The air presses the coin to the glass
coin-inertia-answer-why = Inertia is the keeping of a body’s speed when no other bodies act on it, or their actions balance. The coin lay still, and for it to move the card has to speed it up — by friction. But friction is weak and cannot speed the coin up fast. When the card is drawn slowly, friction keeps up: the coin goes with the card. When the card is snatched, it leaves from under the coin in hundredths of a second — in that time friction hardly moves the coin, and it, staying almost where it was, drops into the glass. The faster the card goes, the less the coin moves. In the same way, when a bus starts sharply, the passengers lean back.
